    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d250">

                <pb facs="0306" n="292" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg292"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.01/157: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-26T11:00:00-03:00">February 26,
                            1944—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:29 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>542. Since my talks with Brazilian and British colleagues (see Embassy’s
                    telegrams 534 February 25, 5 p.m.<anchor n="63" xml:id="d250-f1"/> and 533
                    February 25, 4 p.m.<anchor n="64" xml:id="d250-f2"/>) I have seen Mexican and
                    Chilean Ambassadors.<anchor n="65" xml:id="d250-f3"/> Both were in agreement
                    that situation called for consultation by other American Governments on question
                    of recognition of new regime and I understand are so recommending to their
                    Governments. Member of Peruvian Embassy feels the same way. I expect to see
                        Benavides<anchor n="66" xml:id="d250-f4"/> today. He returned this morning
                    from Uruguay. Chilean Ambassador read me a telegram to his Government in which
                    he had already raised question of recognition. He was much impressed by
                    Under-Secretary’s statement to the press.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d250-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>Repeated to Montevideo, Rio de Janeiro, Santiago, and Lima.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d250-f1" n="63" xml:id="d250-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f2" n="64" xml:id="d250-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg252">p. 252</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f3" n="65" xml:id="d250-f3-content">
                <p>Dario Ojeda and Conrado Ríos Gallardo, respectively.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f4" n="66" xml:id="d250-f4-content">
                <p>Marshal Oscar Benavides, Peruvian Ambassador in Argentina.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d250-f5" n="67" xml:id="d250-f5-content">
                <p>February 25. The statement indicated that the Argentine overnight developments
                    might, because of security considerations, call for an exchange of views among
                    the American Republics. For text of release, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, February 26, 1944, p. 205.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
